<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="E-Waste API - section groups and repeatable workflows">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">E-Waste API functional and concurrency test plan

Source: https://aca-ewaste-dev-api.salmoncoast-1b1c8372.malaysiawest.azurecontainerapps.io/openapi.yaml (the specification used by https://aca-ewaste-dev-api.salmoncoast-1b1c8372.malaysiawest.azurecontainerapps.io/docs/), retrieved 4 October 2026.
OpenAPI 3.0.3, API version 2.0.0. Deployed specification SHA-256: 8bc3b196a02d074456584a8f4671c762472a70d8dc4b14b94de7c65bcb00b4af

QUICK START
Use Apache JMeter 5.6.3 with Java 17, then edit User Defined Variables.
Java 25 fails with the bundled Groovy engine (unsupported class-file version 69).
host includes https://, with no trailing slash. HTTP Request Path uses ${host}/api/v1/... .
Every endpoint remains a native HTTP sampler, with inline Groovy for data preparation and assertions.
No plugins, CSV files, external scripts or hardcoded credentials are required.

Set donor_email/password for Authentication and Batches; add recycler_email/password for
Opportunities; add collector_email/password for Assignments. Use dedicated seeded test users.
Credentials may be set in the GUI, in a private JMeter -q properties file, or in environment
variables EWASTE_DONOR_EMAIL / EWASTE_DONOR_PASSWORD etc. All credentials default to blank.
Test groups fail clearly when an enabled scenario lacks a required credential or fixture.
run_authenticated=false runs only Health and Test, without creating sessions/business rows.
Optional check_ui and check_worker add the provided UI root and worker /readyz health probes.

GROUPS
Health; Test; Authentication; Batches; Opportunities; Assignments; Opportunities FCFS.
Groups run consecutively. Each business group logs in and creates its own fixtures, so groups
can be disabled independently. threads and iterations control independent users/fixtures;
FCFS always uses exactly two threads and one race. Defaults are one user and one iteration.
Blank credentials are never replaced with seed passwords. HTTP timeouts are bounded.
auth_pause_ms defaults to 6500 for the repository&apos;s 10 login/refresh requests per minute
per IP limit. Keep threads=1 for functional runs; higher load may intentionally hit 429.

SCENARIO SWITCHES
run_fcfs=true: requires recycler2 credentials in a DIFFERENT recycler organisation. Both
recyclers must match the configured category, condition, zone, weight and deadline. The two
native claim samplers meet at a synchronizing timer, use the SAME frozen batch version and
epoch but DISTINCT command keys, and must produce exactly one 200 and one 409. Request
intervals must overlap; winner replay must retain its claim/reservation/event identities.
run_isolation=true: requires donor_other credentials in a DIFFERENT donor organisation.
Tests cross-organisation list concealment and object 404 without using existing user batches.
run_recovery=true: after the failed-pickup case, wait for the approved internal recovery service
to restore APPROVED, then use collector2 (a DIFFERENT collector user) to select and hand off.
No recovery endpoint exists in the deployed contract. This option fails on timeout when no
recovery service/operator acts; it never fabricates recovery or writes to the database.

FIXTURES AND ASYNCHRONOUS WORK
New fixtures use notes beginning JMeter:&lt;unique run id&gt;:&lt;scenario&gt;. No existing business row
is mutated. Collection deadline is generated relative to now. Matching requires running
Kafka/matcher, an active rule set, eligible recycler profiles and sufficient capacity.
Assignments require an active collector scope for the selected recycler and fixture zone.
collector_scope_id is extracted from the collector&apos;s scoped GET batch response; it is not
hardcoded. Assignment version is distinct from batch version: If-Match-Version ALWAYS uses
the latest donor batch response. Matching/recovery polling uses timeout and interval settings.
No DELETE/cleanup endpoint is documented: synthetic batches and reservations remain after a
run. Use a disposable environment for frequent runs and retain the fixture IDs from results.

ASSERTIONS AND EXPECTED NEGATIVES
4xx passes ONLY for the named negative scenario and its exact expected status/error code.
Positive requests do not accept 4xx/5xx. Assertions check fields, IDs, state, versions, replay
identity and list shape. A failed step stops that thread before dependent writes.
Assignment selection already returns ACCEPTED. The deprecated /accept endpoint is therefore
tested with an expected 409 on a new selection; no public route creates a legacy PENDING
assignment, so a legacy happy-path accept is not claimed as covered.
The OpenAPI StatusResponse incorrectly requires MySQL/Redis on /healthz; the documented
liveness example is {status:ok}. /readyz separately asserts MySQL and Redis are both ok.

RUN FROM TERMINAL
jmeter -n -t EWaste_API_Test_Plan.jmx -q /path/to/private.properties -l /tmp/ewaste-api.jtl -e -o /tmp/ewaste-api-report
For public checks add -Jrun_authenticated=false. Override host with -Jhost=https://your-api.
Use fresh results/report paths for each run. JMeter may exit zero despite assertion failures:
inspect JTL success=false and the HTML report; the plan does not claim that exit zero means PASS.
Reports omit request/response bodies and headers by default. If you add a View Results Tree
for debugging, authentication requests/responses contain sensitive credentials/tokens.

SCOPE
21 documented method/path operations are represented. Receipt, treatment, evidence and
analytics-result APIs are not in this deployed specification and are not invented here.
The optional UI probe checks availability only, not browser/UI workflows. Worker readiness
is a dependency probe, not a direct test of Python algorithms, Kafka events or database rows.
HTTP replay checks cannot prove internal audit/outbox row counts; keep component evidence.

ENDPOINT INVENTORY
GET /healthz
GET /readyz
GET /api/v1/hello
POST /api/v1/auth/login
POST /api/v1/auth/refresh
POST /api/v1/auth/logout
GET /api/v1/batches
POST /api/v1/batches
GET /api/v1/batches/{batch_id}
PATCH /api/v1/batches/{batch_id}
POST /api/v1/batches/{batch_id}/submit
GET /api/v1/opportunities
GET /api/v1/opportunities/{batch_id}
POST /api/v1/batches/{batch_id}/claim
POST /api/v1/batches/{batch_id}/assignments
GET /api/v1/assignments
GET /api/v1/assignments/{assignment_id}
POST /api/v1/assignments/{assignment_id}/accept
POST /api/v1/assignments/{assignment_id}/reject
POST /api/v1/assignments/{assignment_id}/handoff
POST /api/v1/assignments/{assignment_id}/fail

VALIDATION - 4 OCTOBER 2026
JMeter 5.6.3 / Java 17.0.20.1 loaded and executed this plan against an isolated loopback
contract-shaped mock API. Final full run: 195 samples / 127 HTTP requests, zero failures,
including optional UI/worker probes, isolation, recovery and the two-recycler FCFS race.
An additional two-user / two-iteration run passed 681 samples / 463 HTTP requests.
Fault injection correctly failed on a wrong hello response, changed claim/reservation replay,
unavailable matching and missing credentials. All 21 deployed method/path pairs are present.
These checks validate JMX execution, correlation/extraction, sequencing and assertions.
They do not certify dev&apos;s authentication, database, Kafka or business implementation:
no authenticated or modifying requests were made against dev during preparation.


Swagger refresh 2026-10-08: new groups 08 Processing, 09 Evidence, 10 Analytics, 11 Auditor (all gated OFF initially). Existing groups and credentials are preserved.
Set run_processing=true and donor/recycler/collector credentials to create one complete journey per iteration. processing_fixture: clean/discrepant/partial/missing. wait_for_analytics=true checks the deployed worker; false stops at RECYCLED. run_processing_evidence=true uploads and links optional evidence. Missing outcome is {}, never zero-filled; decimal kg fields are JSON strings.
Standalone groups: run_evidence requires evidence_batch_id (or this run&apos;s last processing batch); empty evidence_file generates a temporary valid PNG. run_analytics requires analytics_service_token plus batch and source event IDs (or group 08 output); run_analytics_result separately opts into d3-v1 service writes and replay. run_auditor requires auditor credentials and audit_batch_id (or group 08 output).
Groups run sequentially. Automatic ID handoff refers to the last group 08 iteration only; use explicit IDs for multi-thread tests. No IDs carry over to the next run. Inspect Share processing identities for batch/source event IDs. Existing access tokens can expire during long runs; keep functional runs short. HTTP timeouts bound each request as well as the poll deadline.
Only upload synthetic test evidence. The default generated PNG needs no external fixture. Credentials remain configurable; do not share a saved plan containing real secrets. This plan prepares business mutations when enabled; it does not run itself.

Local validation: JMeter 5.6.3 on Java 17. Functional Groovy scripts should be run with a compatible JDK (Java 17 was used); Java 25 is incompatible with the bundled Groovy version. No authenticated dev workflows were executed during this update.

SPRINT 4 EXTENSION
Added 15 October 2026 against local main-repository contracts. See artifacts/jmeter/README.md.
Groups S4 12-19 extend the existing plan; run_s4 defaults false. The CLI runner selects only these groups.
Claim race, same-assignment handoff race, independent five-wave processing burst, and custody reads
use native HTTP samplers. Setup/polls/replays/expected negatives have separate labels. Inline Groovy
prepares data and asserts results; no HTTP request is issued from Groovy. All durable fixtures are
created through APIs and tagged S4LD. SQL reconciliation remains required to prove exact row counts.
Credentials and explicit legacy fixture IDs now have empty defaults. Configure them externally.
</stringProp>
